---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import MailLink from '../components/MailLink.astro';
---
<Base title="Cookie Policy — Brian J. Mueller"
      description="This website sets no cookies. The one third-party script it loads, what is stored in your browser, and how to remove it.">
  <section class="wrap stack stack-l">
    <div class="stack stack-m prose legal">
      <div class="eyebrow">Legal</div>
      <h1>Cookie Policy</h1>
      <hr class="rule-short" />
      <p class="small">Last updated: September 18, 2026</p>

      <p><b>This website sets no cookies.</b> Not for advertising, not for analytics, not for
         anything else — and no third party sets one through it either. There is nothing to
         accept or refuse, which is why you were not shown a cookie banner.</p>

      <p>It does count visitors, and it does so without a cookie. The next section says exactly
         how, because &ldquo;no cookies&rdquo; and &ldquo;no analytics&rdquo; are different claims
         and only the first one is true here.</p>

      <p>That has not always been true. Earlier versions of this site ran on Squarespace and used
         Google Analytics, and an earlier version of this page described their cookies. Neither is
         in use any more, and this page has been rewritten to describe what actually runs.</p>

      <h2>The one script that is not ours</h2>
      <p>Every page loads a small counting script from Cloudflare, our host, called Cloudflare Web
         Analytics. It tells us how many people read a page, which page they came from, and how
         quickly the page loaded. It is the only third-party file this site loads.</p>
      <p>It is worth being exact about what it does and does not do, because the two are easy to
         confuse:</p>
      <ul>
      <li>It sets <b>no cookie</b>, and stores nothing in your browser — not in local storage, not
          in session storage, not anywhere else. We checked this on the live site rather than
          taking it on trust.</li>
      <li>It builds <b>no profile and no identifier</b> for you, and it is not shared across
          websites, so it cannot follow you from here to anywhere else.</li>
      <li>It does see your <b>IP address</b>, as any request to any website does, and the page you
          are on. Cloudflare uses the address to work out a rough country and then discards it;
          we never see it.</li>
      <li>What reaches us is <b>a count</b>. We cannot pick you out of it, and there is no screen
          anywhere that would let us try.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>If you would rather it did not run, any content blocker will stop it, and the site works
         exactly the same without it. Nothing on this site is gated on it loading.</p>

      <h2>What is stored in your browser</h2>
      <p>One thing, and only if you ask for it:</p>
      <ul>
      <li><b>Your light or dark preference.</b> If you use the theme button in the page header, the
          choice is written to your browser's local storage under the key <code>theme</code>. It is
          read before the page paints so the site does not flash the wrong colours at you.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Local storage is not a cookie: it is never attached to a request, so it is never sent to
         this website or to anyone else. It stays on the one device and in the one browser you set
         it in. If you never touch the theme button, nothing is stored at all — the counting
         script above adds nothing here.</p>

      <h3>Removing it</h3>
      <p>The button below deletes the <code>theme</code> key and puts this site back to following
         whatever your device is set to. It works immediately, here, without changing anything else.</p>

      <div class="btn-row">
        <button id="theme-reset" class="btn" type="button">Use my device setting</button>
      </div>
      <p class="small" id="theme-reset-status" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>

      <p>Clearing site data for this website in your browser does the same thing, along with
         anything else a site might have stored — here, that is only this one key.</p>
      <ul>
      <li><a href="https://support.google.com/chrome/answer/95647">Google Chrome</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/105082">Safari</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/clear-cookies-and-site-data-firefox">Mozilla Firefox</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-edge/delete-cookies-in-microsoft-edge-63947406-40ac-c3b8-57b9-2a946a29ae09">Microsoft Edge</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://support.brave.app/hc/en-us/articles/360048833872-How-Do-I-Clear-Cookies-And-Site-Data-In-Brave">Brave</a></li>
      </ul>

      <p>One thing worth saying plainly, because this page said the opposite until
         19 September 2026: <b>switching the theme button in the header back does not remove
         anything.</b> It stores the other choice. Once you have used it at all, the site follows
         what you stored rather than what your device says, until you clear it here or in your
         browser.</p>

      <h2>What the host records</h2>
      <p>The site is delivered by Cloudflare. Like every web host, Cloudflare's servers write a log
         line for each request — the IP address it came from, the time, the page asked for, and the
         browser string — which is what lets it serve the pages and absorb abuse. That happens on
         Cloudflare's side of the connection, uses no cookie, and cannot follow you to other
         websites. We do not keep a separate copy of it and we do not use it to build a profile of
         you. Our <a href="/privacy-policy">Privacy Policy</a> covers it in more detail.</p>

      <h2>Global Privacy Control</h2>
      <p>Some browsers and extensions send a Global Privacy Control signal, which asks a site not to
         sell or share personal information. We do not sell or share personal information in the
         first place, so there is nothing for the signal to switch off. Where the law requires us to
         honour it, we will.</p>

      <h2>If this changes</h2>
      <p>If this site ever adds something that sets a cookie — a payment step for the writing
         retreat is the likeliest candidate — this page will be updated to name it before it goes
         live, and the date at the top will change.</p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>Questions about this policy: <MailLink user="bdrive.co" domain="gmail.com"><a href="/contact">write to Brian</a></MailLink>, or use
         <a href="/contact">the contact page</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
<script is:inline>
  // The control this page promises. It removes the stored preference and the
  // explicit override, then tells the header toggle to repaint itself.
  //
  // This exists because the policy described a reset the site did not perform:
  // the header toggle calls localStorage.setItem on every click and has no path
  // that removes the key, so "switch it back" stored the opposite value instead
  // of restoring system behaviour. Found by the Codex audit, 19 September 2026.
  (function () {
    var btn = document.getElementById("theme-reset");
    var out = document.getElementById("theme-reset-status");
    if (!btn || !out) return;

    function stored() {
      try { return localStorage.getItem("theme"); } catch (e) { return null; }
    }
    // The button is never disabled. Disabling it after a click drops keyboard
    // focus to the top of the document, and clearing a key that is not there is
    // a no-op anyway - so it is always safe to press and the line below always
    // says what is true.
    function describe(msg) {
      var v = stored();
      out.textContent = msg ? msg
        : (v === "light" || v === "dark")
          ? "Right now this browser is storing: " + v + "."
          : "Nothing is stored — this site is already following your device setting.";
    }
    btn.addEventListener("click", function () {
      var had = stored();
      try { localStorage.removeItem("theme"); } catch (e) {}
      document.documentElement.removeAttribute("data-theme");
      document.dispatchEvent(new Event("themecleared"));
      describe(had ? "Removed. This site now follows your device setting again."
                   : "Nothing was stored — this site was already following your device setting.");
    });
    // Keep the description true while the visitor is on this page: the header
    // toggle sits a few centimetres above and changes exactly what this reports.
    document.addEventListener("themechanged", function () { describe(); });
    describe();
  })();
</script>
</Base>
